  "note": "Judged blind on 18 staged pairs, critic prompts generated mechanically from the task set. First capture carrying the `record` and `instrument` blocks, so a critic could distinguish \"the record settles this and they agree\" from \"no record could settle this\" from \"the harness could not measure it\". Critics used that distinction throughout, counting plan-step counts and sandbox timings unsettleable rather than agreed.",
  "correction": "V1 is recorded twice. As reported, its two cross-cutting columns went to B. But the same critic scored V1’s TASK column a tie on the ground that the entire delta was one space character the model typed — one arm wrote \"165 °F\", the other \"165°F\", and the backing checker matches literally. The protocol’s stated rule is that a difference which would vanish under identical tokens is not a difference. Applied uniformly, V1 ties in all three columns. `columns` carries the corrected reading; `columnsAsReported` carries the critic’s raw verdicts. The difference is one win in each cross-cutting column.",
  "caveats": [
    "The judging is not evidence that the two builds are equivalent. Sixteen of eighteen tasks tied, and the critics repeatedly said the behaviour under test was never exercised — TTU3’s claim check could not run because every search failed, VC1’s token was never repeated back by either model, PT3 never completed a step.",
    "Both cross-cutting columns came back 0-0-18 under the corrected reading. That is either two clean builds or two columns with nothing to bite on; the statement counts the critics reported beside each verdict are what distinguishes those, and they are in the reports rather than in this file."
  ],
